On Thu, 15 Oct 2026 16:39:48 -0700, Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com> wrote:
> The UPDATING approach seems risky. I have to keep an eye on the ports
> tree, watch out for every release, and upgrade each one. If I miss a
> required one, I'm hosed, right?

Yes, and it is worse than a missed note. By the time you see the
problem, pkg has already replaced the old package. pkg shows a
`type: upgrade` message only after it installs the new one
(libpkg/pkg_add.c:1805-1821, pkg at 34da047). GitLab's own
schema_version_check then refuses to migrate the database, so the
service is down on code that will not run against your data.

There is also nothing to go back to. pkg.freebsd.org keeps only
latest, the current quarterly, and the release_N snapshots. A named
old quarter such as FreeBSD:14:amd64/2025Q4/ returns 404. The forum
thread "GitLab skip major version update not possible but cannot find
old mid versions packages" [a] (May 2025) is this exact case: 15 to 17
with no 16 package anywhere. The advice there was to check out an old
ports revision and build it in a jail.

I need to correct my last reply. I said nothing in pkg could block an
upgrade, but that is too strong. pkg has no declarative rule, but a
pre-install script can refuse. A nonzero exit from pkg-pre-install, or
an error from pkg-pre-install.lua, is fatal (libpkg/scripts.c:309-314,
lua_scripts.c:113-121). Both run before anything is extracted
(pkg_add.c:1700-1708), and the framework already packages them
(Mk/bsd.port.mk:1940). No port in the tree uses one to check versions.
GitLab's own deb and rpm packages do, though. Their preinst sets
MIN_VERSION and exits 1 with "It is required to upgrade to the latest
${MIN_VERSION}.x version first" [b].

> Here's what I have in mind:
>
> - a slave port (I think that's the name / mechanism in ports?) or a
>   virtual port that does nothing but include info about how to upgrade

Slave port is the right name; the mechanism is MASTERDIR. For example,
games/openbor3482 pins an older games/openbor with its own
PORTVERSION, PKGNAMESUFFIX, and distinfo.

I would not put the upgrade info in a separate port, though. It is
only useful at the moment pkg is about to upgrade, and the only scripts
that run then belong to the package being upgraded. It belongs in that
package's guard, so the refusal itself names the stop and says how to
install it.

> - a port for every stop point, or maybe a flavor for every stop point

I recommend a slave port per stop. A flavor can carry its own
DISTVERSION if you set it by hand, as devel/libclc does with
DISTVERSION=${DISTVERSION_${FLAVOR}}. But the flavor helpers vary
only DESCR, PLIST, and the package name (Mk/bsd.port.mk:1486-1497).
All flavors share one distinfo, one files/, and one dependency list,
and those are exactly what differ between stops. A slave owns its
distinfo and files/, and you can delete it without touching the
master.

> - package message that shows how to upgrade

Keep it, but as the second line of defense. It is shown after the
install, so it cannot stop anything. The text that matters is the
guard's refusal. A pkg pull request adding a message shown before
installation [c] has been open since 2020.

> - stop points are retired after some period of time (6 months, a year?)

I answer this at the end, because the guard changes what retirement
costs.

> A challenge is that we don't necessarily know what is a stop point until
> after the fact. It's only when a new release comes out that we know it's
> a stop point.

A guard handles this well, because the floor belongs to the newer
package. When N+1 ships saying "you must come from N", the port update
to N+1 adds "floor N". N's package never changes, and nobody has to
predict stops. Every system I found that enforces stops puts the check
in the newer version:
- GitLab's preinst;
- Nextcloud's $OC_VersionCanBeUpgradedFrom allowlist [d], which its
  Docker entrypoint enforces too;
- OpenShift's update graph, which adds and blocks upgrade edges after
  release in a separate data repo [e].

The hard part is the other side. Once you learn that N is a stop, a
package for N has to exist. If the port has already moved past N, the
only way back is to build N from ports git. Every quarterly branch
since 2014Q1 is still there, so this is always possible, but it is not
a pkg install.

For GitLab most of the surprise is gone. Since 17.5 its stops fall at
x.2, x.5, x.8, and x.11, "to provide a predictable upgrade schedule"
[f]. They are listed in config/upgrade_path.yml in the GitLab source
[g], so a port could read its floor from the distfile instead of
keeping it by hand. I have not checked that www/gitlab's distfile
carries that file.

SigNoz stops are not predictable. v0.86 is mandatory for its SQL
migrations [h], and v0.113 replaced the schema migrator [i]. SigNoz
publishes an Upgrade Path Tool, but I found no machine-readable list
behind it.

> I want to come up with a way that is operationally responsible. The
> reality of the current way is that you cannot run GitLab unless you
> build it yourself and stop at versions - otherwise there's too much risk
> in missing a necessary upgrade.

Agreed. FreeBSD users report the same thing, in the thread above and
in "pkg upgrade package to intermediate version" [j], about the 13.12
stop. The answer there was that pkg offers only what the repo has.

> Do some more research to see if anyone has thought about this in more
> depth than just some notes in UPDATING. Also research how this problem
> is approached in other ecosystems.

Within FreeBSD I found nothing deeper. I checked ports at 8d0e75871
(2026-10-15), pkg's source and GitHub issues, Bugzilla, and the forums.
For the mailing lists I relied on web searches and did not read the
archives directly, so treat that "nothing" as weak. The closest items:
- pkg issue #1573, multiple versions of a package in one repo, is
  still open [k];
- pkg PR #1910 [c] is the pre-install message above;
- Bug 247616, a broken gitlab-ce 12.9 to 13.1 upgrade, ended with a
  pointer to upstream's per-step guides.
Every versioned port in the tree (mongodbNN, postgresqlNN, and so on)
exists for parallel support. None is kept as an upgrade stop.

Other ecosystems, from most to least relevant:

- GitLab does it in three layers. A data file lists the stops [g].
  The omnibus preinst refuses a skipped stop [b]. The app refuses to
  migrate a schema older than its oldest shipped migration. Every
  version stays on packages.gitlab.com, and users pin each hop with
  `apt install gitlab-ee=<version>`.

- NixOS is the closest match to a ports tree. Nextcloud is packaged
  as nextcloudNN, one package per major, and the default comes from
  system.stateVersion, so a rebuild never moves you a major on its
  own. The module warns which major comes next. When a major is
  removed, its name stays behind as an alias that fails with the
  stop, e.g. "if you were on Nextcloud 31 you have to upgrade to
  Nextcloud 32 first on 26.05" [l]. A retired stop still tells you
  where it is.

- Nextcloud also refuses multi-major jumps in the app [d] and in its
  Docker image. Its snap has one track per major (27/stable), so a
  user steps through with `snap refresh --channel=`.

- OpenShift's Cincinnati is a formal upgrade graph. Releases are
  nodes and allowed upgrades are edges. The client walks it one hop at
  a time, and edges can be blocked after release [e]. Kubernetes OLM
  does the same for operators with `replaces` and `skipRange` [m].

- Debian, Fedora, and Arch make the distribution release the stop,
  and you may not skip releases. Majors that need it ship as parallel
  packages (postgresql-NN; Arch's postgresql-old-upgrade always runs
  one major behind). A Debian preinst receives the old version as $2,
  so it could refuse, but I found no package that does. RPM %pre can
  refuse but is not told the old version. Homebrew has foo@X
  formulae and no stop mechanism.

I found no general write-up of the problem: nothing on LWN and no
talk. The best design discussion is GitLab's own, in the merge request
that introduced scheduled stops [n] and the issue on keeping stops
from piling up [o].

> I would like to think through this
> and see if there's a way to provide a reliable upgrade path for tools
> that require upgrade stops.

I propose three parts.

1. Refuse in the main port. Add a pkg-pre-install guard that compares
   the version the data was last migrated at with the port's floor.
   If the data is older, it exits 1 with a message naming the stop
   and how to get it. "Hosed" becomes "pkg upgrade failed and nothing
   changed". The floor is a single Makefile variable, substituted into
   the script with SUB_FILES.

   Where the old version comes from matters. pkg does not tell scripts
   which version they replace. It knows (pkg_jobs.c:2086) but exports
   only PKG_UPGRADE=true (scripts.c:106-107). In a split upgrade the
   old package is deleted before the new pre-install runs
   (pkg_add.c:1741-1745), so a file from the old plist may be gone
   already. I would therefore keep a stamp in the state directory,
   e.g. /var/db/signoz/version, written by the rc script after a
   successful migration and left out of the plist. It records what
   actually matters, which is the version the data is at. It also
   catches a reinstall over old data after a pkg delete.

   It has limits. `pkg upgrade -I` or RUN_SCRIPTS=no bypasses it, so
   it is a guard, not a lock. From my reading of pkg_jobs.c:2208-2248,
   pkg stops the job at the first failure but does not roll back
   packages already upgraded in that run. GitLab's dependencies, like
   gitaly, are separate packages and could move anyway; SigNoz is a
   single package, so that does not apply. I could not test any of
   this, because pkg needs root even for a scratch rootdir, so all of
   it is read from source.

2. Supply stops as slave ports, as you suggested. For example,
   net-mgmt/signoz086 would set MASTERDIR=../signoz and carry its own
   DISTVERSION, distinfo, PKGNAMESUFFIX, and CONFLICTS_INSTALL.
   Binary packages carry no conflicts and pkg never swaps one package
   name for another, so the hop is explicit, and the guard can print
   it verbatim:

     pkg delete signoz && pkg install signoz086
     service signoz start # migrates; stamp moves to 0.86
     pkg delete signoz086 && pkg install signoz

   That works for SigNoz, whose Go modules are vendored per version,
   so a stop builds from its own distfiles. It does not work for
   GitLab. A GitLab stop needs the rubygem ports as they were at the
   time, and the tree has moved on, so each stop port would drag a
   frozen copy of hundreds of dependencies with it.

   For GitLab, and for the lab in general, a stop repo is the better
   fit. Your poudriere builds the port from the quarterly branch or
   ports commit where it sat at the stop version, into its own repo,
   under the same package name. The hop is then `pkg install -r
   <stop-repo> gitlab-ce` followed by a normal upgrade. There are no
   name swaps, and the dependencies match their tree. This is what
   GitLab's package server and NixOS's release branches amount to: an
   older snapshot kept on purpose. The official ports tree cannot
   offer it, but the guard can still name the branch to build.

3. Retire stops with a tombstone. When a stop port is removed, the
   guard keeps refusing, and its message names the stop and how to
   build it, e.g. "build net-mgmt/signoz from branch 2026Q3". That is
   the NixOS alias. After retirement nobody is hosed; they just take
   a slower path. So the retirement period is a convenience, not a
   safety margin. I suggest counting it in quarterly branches, not
   months, and keeping a stop until four have shipped past it, about a
   year. At GitLab's four stops a year, that means at most four stop
   ports at once, which is another reason to use repos for GitLab.

My recommendation is to try parts 1 and 3 on net-mgmt/signoz first.
You accepted v10 [1], and its rc script is already where migrations
are about to land, per the collector plan in "Re: [freebsd-ports PATCH
v10] net-mgmt/signoz: Observability platform for logs, metrics, and
traces" [2]. That plan runs "migrate sync check" from start_precmd, and
writing the stamp belongs right next to it. The current floor would be
empty, since the port starts at 0.145.0, so the patch would add the
mechanism and a test of the refusal in your poudriere jail, where pkg
runs as root. Part 2 waits until a real stop shows up.

What I need from you is a choice:
(a) fold the guard and stamp into the collector series;
(b) send them as their own patch on top of v10;
(c) prototype on a toy port first, outside signoz.
I recommend (b). It keeps the collector series about the collector, and
it lets you judge the mechanism by itself.

